\DOC DISCHARGE_CONCRETE_MEMACCESS_INBOUNDS_TAC

\TYPE {DISCHARGE_CONCRETE_MEMACCESS_INBOUNDS_TAC : tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Discharges a memory-access-in-bounds goal about a concrete event trace, either
proving it outright or reducing it to a residual {ALL}-over-events condition.

\DESCRIBE
Takes no arguments. It acts on the conclusion only, leaving the assumptions {A}
untouched. In most applications it closes the goal outright, producing no
subgoals; this happens both for conclusions of the form
{memaccess_inbounds t u1 u2} whose trace argument is a fully concrete list of
load/store/jump events, and for conclusions already stated as a conjunction of
{ALL}/{EX} containment conditions over read and write region lists. In the
remaining applications, where the trace contains opaque sub-traces (named trace
functions, {APPEND}s of them, or {ENUMERATEL} bodies) that cannot be fully
enumerated, it rewrites the goal in place into a single residual subgoal: an
explicit per-event containment predicate applied to the same (possibly slightly
normalised, e.g. {CONS}/{APPEND}-expanded) trace, with the region bounds from
the original goal inlined as {contained_modulo} disjuncts.
{
       A ?- memaccess_inbounds t u1 u2
     ==================================  DISCHARGE_CONCRETE_MEMACCESS_INBOUNDS_TAC
       (no goals)
}
\noindent or
{
       A ?- memaccess_inbounds t u1 u2
     =============================================================================  DISCHARGE_CONCRETE_MEMACCESS_INBOUNDS_TAC
       A ?- ALL (\e. match e with EventLoad ... | EventStore ... | _ -> true) t
}

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only these two outcomes, and all inputs are goals
about memory-access-in-bounds properties of x86 event traces.

\ENDDOC
